WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
- In reality, entrepreneurs do

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.894
everything they can to minimize risk.

00:00:05.894 --> 00:00:08.061
The first thing you want to do

00:00:08.061 --> 00:00:11.756
is define the market
that you're going after.

00:00:11.756 --> 00:00:14.657
That it's not the size of the circle

00:00:14.657 --> 00:00:16.709
that matters but knowing its boundaries.

00:00:16.709 --> 00:00:18.572
So if you don't truly think it's something

00:00:18.572 --> 00:00:20.633
that you would yourself be a customer of,

00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:21.883
don't try to pitch it.

00:00:21.883 --> 00:00:23.630
The single biggest advantage

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:26.862
a value investor has is not IQ.

00:00:26.862 --> 00:00:29.073
It's patience and waiting.

00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:30.513
Avoid the use of spreadsheets

00:00:30.513 --> 00:00:31.960
and avoid precision thinking.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.289
And that's no fun, it's not fun

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:35.670
to hit your head against a brick wall.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:37.044
So what you want is a business

00:00:37.044 --> 00:00:39.925
that has a deep moat with
lots of piranha in it,

00:00:39.925 --> 00:00:42.819
and that's getting deeper by the day.

00:00:42.819 --> 00:00:44.726
I'm flexible, I think it's very important

00:00:44.726 --> 00:00:46.953
in investing to be a learning machine.

00:00:46.953 --> 00:00:49.039
Pay a lot of close attention

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:51.532
to the feedback you get from the people

00:00:51.532 --> 00:00:53.462
who are listening to the pitch.

00:00:53.462 --> 00:00:55.394
- He's an Indian-American investor,

00:00:55.394 --> 00:00:57.445
businessman, and philanthropist.

00:00:57.445 --> 00:00:59.489
He sold his company TransTech Inc.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:01.684
in 2000 for twenty million dollars.

00:01:01.684 --> 00:01:05.416
Today he is a managing partner
of Pabrai Investment Funds.

00:01:05.416 --> 00:01:06.800
He's Mohnish Pabrai and here

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.661
are his top ten rules for success.

00:01:08.661 --> 00:01:11.857
(musical zoom)

00:01:11.857 --> 00:01:14.802
- People think that
entrepreneurs take risk

00:01:14.802 --> 00:01:17.398
and they get rewarded
because they take risk.

00:01:17.398 --> 00:01:19.739
In reality, entrepreneurs do

00:01:19.739 --> 00:01:22.822
everything they can to minimize risk.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.834
They're not interested in taking risks.

00:01:26.834 --> 00:01:28.666
They want free lunches, and they

00:01:28.666 --> 00:01:30.570
go after free lunches, and so

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.601
if you study any number of entrepreneurs,

00:01:33.601 --> 00:01:37.101
from Ray Kroc to Herb Schultz at Starbucks

00:01:38.175 --> 00:01:41.776
to even Buffett and Munger and so on,

00:01:41.776 --> 00:01:43.415
what you'll find is that they have

00:01:43.415 --> 00:01:47.183
repeatedly made bets
which are low risk bets

00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:50.049
which have high return possibilities.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:52.385
So they are not going
high risk, high return.

00:01:52.385 --> 00:01:54.646
They're going low risk, high return.

00:01:54.646 --> 00:01:57.941
And even with Bill Gates, for example,

00:01:57.941 --> 00:02:00.391
the total amount of capital that ever went

00:02:00.391 --> 00:02:03.172
into Microsoft was less than 30 thousand

00:02:03.172 --> 00:02:05.226
between the time it started and today.

00:02:05.226 --> 00:02:06.915
That's a total amount of capital

00:02:06.915 --> 00:02:08.498
that went into the company, so

00:02:08.498 --> 00:02:12.259
Microsoft, you cannot say
was a high risk venture,

00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:15.077
because there was no capital deployed.

00:02:15.077 --> 00:02:16.681
But it had high uncertainty.

00:02:16.681 --> 00:02:19.372
Bill Gates could have gone bankrupt

00:02:19.372 --> 00:02:20.633
or Bill Gates could have ended up

00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:23.509
the wealthiest person on the Forbes 400.

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:25.618
And he ended up at the extreme end

00:02:25.618 --> 00:02:27.605
of the bell curve, and that's fine,

00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:30.697
but he did not take risk to get there.

00:02:30.697 --> 00:02:32.455
He was comfortable with uncertainty,

00:02:32.455 --> 00:02:35.158
so entrepreneurs are great at dealing

00:02:35.158 --> 00:02:37.803
with uncertainty and also very good

00:02:37.803 --> 00:02:39.396
at minimizing risk, that's the

00:02:39.396 --> 00:02:41.502
classic, great entrepreneur.

00:02:41.502 --> 00:02:44.410
If you look at any market, any product,

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:46.781
any market, they will typically be

00:02:46.781 --> 00:02:48.884
three or four players at the most

00:02:48.884 --> 00:02:52.498
that control 80 percent of that market,

00:02:52.498 --> 00:02:55.667
and it could be making airplanes,

00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:58.417
or it could be making hair clips,

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.581
you can pretty much go across the board.

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.982
It could be making
software, anything you want,

00:03:03.982 --> 00:03:07.466
and I found that stunning, and if you

00:03:07.466 --> 00:03:10.199
invert that, so one of my heroes

00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:12.031
Charlie Munger says, every time you

00:03:12.031 --> 00:03:15.184
encounter something always invert

00:03:15.184 --> 00:03:17.614
the logic to try to get some other place.

00:03:17.614 --> 00:03:19.967
So if you invert that principle,

00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:21.004
first of all if you agree with

00:03:21.004 --> 00:03:24.090
that principle, then the inversion will be

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:26.954
that if you're starting a business

00:03:26.954 --> 00:03:31.121
and you say that this is a
ten billion dollar market

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.120
and I just need ten percent of it

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.088
and I'll be a billion dollar company

00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:41.304
well, right there, you're off,

00:03:41.304 --> 00:03:42.993
because the inversion will be that

00:03:42.993 --> 00:03:45.751
if you're going after a market,

00:03:45.751 --> 00:03:48.834
your approach has to be, how do I get

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.042
sixty percent of that market, and so

00:03:53.042 --> 00:03:54.685
if your starting point is that

00:03:54.685 --> 00:03:57.078
I'm going to take two
percent of this market

00:03:57.078 --> 00:03:58.996
or five percent of this market,

00:03:58.996 --> 00:04:01.330
what you have not done is you have not

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.720
segmented that market properly.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.858
And that is a fatal flaw, so the first

00:04:07.858 --> 00:04:11.275
thing you want to do is define the market

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.723
that you're going after, and after

00:04:14.723 --> 00:04:17.640
you defined it, if you don't get to

00:04:18.772 --> 00:04:21.236
very clear cut game plan, to get

00:04:21.236 --> 00:04:24.200
50, 60 percent market share, then

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.195
don't even try, because either

00:04:26.195 --> 00:04:27.772
you have not done your job correctly

00:04:27.772 --> 00:04:30.355
or you don't have anything to offer

00:04:30.355 --> 00:04:33.012
that is going to be that
compelling to people.

00:04:33.012 --> 00:04:35.553
I think another very important

00:04:35.553 --> 00:04:38.627
trait for successful investing

00:04:38.627 --> 00:04:41.694
is to stay within your
circle of competence.

00:04:41.694 --> 00:04:43.991
So I think Buffett always says

00:04:43.991 --> 00:04:47.442
that it's not the size of
the circle that matters

00:04:47.442 --> 00:04:50.517
but knowing its boundaries
is absolutely critical,

00:04:50.517 --> 00:04:53.017
and Charlie Munger talks about

00:04:55.406 --> 00:04:57.708
that he used a couple of examples.

00:04:57.708 --> 00:05:01.001
One is, he says, that if in a small town

00:05:01.001 --> 00:05:03.918
you bought the Mcdonalds franchise,

00:05:07.049 --> 00:05:08.540
you bought the Ford dealership--

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:09.773
- [Man] Gas station?

00:05:09.773 --> 00:05:12.706
- Ford dealership, gas
station not so good.

00:05:12.706 --> 00:05:15.519
And gas station we'll
leave for the Patels,

00:05:15.519 --> 00:05:18.165
low cost operations, okay, and then

00:05:18.165 --> 00:05:21.947
you want the best classy office building

00:05:21.947 --> 00:05:24.816
and you want the best
residential buidling,

00:05:24.816 --> 00:05:26.571
so if you got these four assets,

00:05:26.571 --> 00:05:28.318
and you don't need to own them completely,

00:05:28.318 --> 00:05:30.631
you could own 20 percent of each of them.

00:05:30.631 --> 00:05:33.164
So his perspective was
that if in Peoria, Illinois

00:05:33.164 --> 00:05:35.360
you own these four assets and you just

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:37.224
sat on them for your whole life,

00:05:37.224 --> 00:05:38.911
you would end up probably quite wealthy.

00:05:38.911 --> 00:05:41.413
And if so you think about it from a

00:05:41.413 --> 00:05:43.701
modern portfolio theory point of view,

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:45.748
you would say, well,
you're not diversified,

00:05:45.748 --> 00:05:47.918
everything is in one geography,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:05:47.918 --> 00:05:50.335
Yeah, but the odds still work out.

00:05:50.335 --> 00:05:55.332
And in fact he has a friend
here in, near Stanford,

00:05:55.332 --> 00:05:58.577
John Arrillaga, who,
all he did, was bought

00:05:58.577 --> 00:06:02.906
real estate within a mile
of the Stanford campus.

00:06:02.906 --> 00:06:05.418
And I think Arrillaga is a billionaire.

00:06:05.418 --> 00:06:09.244
And so what is Arrillaga's
circle of competence?

00:06:09.244 --> 00:06:13.411
It's this small, so the size
of the circle is not relevant.

00:06:15.436 --> 00:06:18.950
Staying inside the circle
is very fundamental.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:23.175
Do not try to sell something
that you wouldn't buy yourself.

00:06:23.175 --> 00:06:26.147
So if you don't truly think it's something

00:06:26.147 --> 00:06:28.319
that you would yourself be a customer of,

00:06:28.319 --> 00:06:30.101
don't try to pitch it, don't be

00:06:30.101 --> 00:06:32.132
a used car salesman, so please

00:06:32.132 --> 00:06:33.557
make sure that whatever you're trying

00:06:33.557 --> 00:06:36.130
to come up with is compelling enough

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.082
that you would yourself be deeply

00:06:38.082 --> 00:06:40.668
interested in buying that product.

00:06:40.668 --> 00:06:43.347
So Charlie Munger likes to say that

00:06:43.347 --> 00:06:46.746
you don't make money when you buy stocks

00:06:46.746 --> 00:06:50.242
and you don't make money
when you sell stocks.

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:53.065
You make money by waiting,

00:06:53.065 --> 00:06:56.742
and so the biggest, the
single biggest advantage

00:06:56.742 --> 00:06:59.418
a value investor has is not IQ.

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:02.341
It's patience, and waiting, waiting for

00:07:02.341 --> 00:07:04.026
the right pitch, and waiting

00:07:04.026 --> 00:07:06.518
for many years for the right pitch.

00:07:06.518 --> 00:07:08.474
- So what's that saying of Pascal

00:07:08.474 --> 00:07:11.535
that you like about just sitting, and?

00:07:11.535 --> 00:07:13.610
- Yeah, all man's misery stems from

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:18.042
his inability to sit in a
room alone and do nothing,

00:07:18.042 --> 00:07:20.375
and all I do to adapt Pascal

00:07:21.431 --> 00:07:24.979
is all investment managers' miseries

00:07:24.979 --> 00:07:27.295
stem from their inability to sit alone

00:07:27.295 --> 00:07:29.190
in a room and do nothing.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.857
The way to really look
at investing is that,

00:07:36.106 --> 00:07:38.772
when you look at, again,
a business like Google

00:07:38.772 --> 00:07:41.374
or Microsoft or BugShare, you really

00:07:41.374 --> 00:07:43.021
have to put yourself in the shoes

00:07:43.021 --> 00:07:45.127
of the people running the business

00:07:45.127 --> 00:07:48.433
and you do ask yourself how
do they run the business.

00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:51.080
Do they run it through Excel spreadsheets,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:52.835
or how do they run it?

00:07:52.835 --> 00:07:55.616
And I would bet that most of these

00:07:55.616 --> 00:07:57.920
businesses are run in a manner where

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.185
the founders or CEOs are really looking

00:08:01.185 --> 00:08:03.378
at kind of three to five variables

00:08:03.378 --> 00:08:05.991
that dominate most of their thinking

00:08:05.991 --> 00:08:07.721
and outcome and direction, and so

00:08:07.721 --> 00:08:10.673
as an investor you've got to hone in

00:08:10.673 --> 00:08:13.066
on those same variables, so if you

00:08:13.066 --> 00:08:14.718
can get to the same variables,

00:08:14.718 --> 00:08:16.055
if you want to invest in Microsoft,

00:08:16.055 --> 00:08:17.589
you can get to the same variables

00:08:17.589 --> 00:08:19.315
that they are using, and if you're

00:08:19.315 --> 00:08:20.790
Google, you get the same variables

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:23.313
that they are using, then you are

00:08:23.313 --> 00:08:25.063
getting very close to

00:08:25.971 --> 00:08:28.639
trying to figure out what
the business might do

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:31.531
and from there you can extrapolate

00:08:31.531 --> 00:08:35.375
whether it's underpriced
or fairly priced and so on.

00:08:35.375 --> 00:08:37.159
So that's all I would suggest is

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:38.730
avoid the use of spreadsheets and

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:39.949
avoid precision thinking.

00:08:39.949 --> 00:08:42.199
The stronger the marketing,

00:08:44.532 --> 00:08:48.699
the weaker the sales engine
can be, so I do not consider

00:08:49.911 --> 00:08:51.828
myself a good salesman.

00:08:53.099 --> 00:08:56.070
I consider myself a poor salesman.

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.237
I consider myself a pretty
strong marketing person.

00:09:01.119 --> 00:09:05.202
And so if you have done
your marketing homework,

00:09:05.202 --> 00:09:08.035
you can be a leper and make sales.

00:09:09.073 --> 00:09:11.447
And a standing example of that is

00:09:11.447 --> 00:09:14.020
someone in front of you, because

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:16.520
basically my approach to sales

00:09:18.588 --> 00:09:21.354
would be 180 degrees approach to

00:09:21.354 --> 00:09:24.704
your traditional used car salesman

00:09:24.704 --> 00:09:27.473
schmoozing model and golf
courses and all that,

00:09:27.473 --> 00:09:29.787
none of that is of much interest to me.

00:09:29.787 --> 00:09:32.287
So the stronger the marketing,

00:09:33.275 --> 00:09:37.220
the less important the
sales engine becomes.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:40.154
So it is very important in my opinion

00:09:40.154 --> 00:09:43.307
to spend incredible amounts of time

00:09:43.307 --> 00:09:46.640
on the marketing aspect of the business.

00:09:47.642 --> 00:09:49.648
Because if you don't spend that time

00:09:49.648 --> 00:09:51.544
on that aspect of it, you will spend

00:09:51.544 --> 00:09:54.296
50 extra that time on that sales end

00:09:54.296 --> 00:09:56.797
and hit your head against a brick wall.

00:09:56.797 --> 00:09:59.133
And that's no fun, it's not fun

00:09:59.133 --> 00:10:00.958
to hit your head against a brick wall.

00:10:00.958 --> 00:10:04.352
So it's good not to go
there, so I just feel

00:10:04.352 --> 00:10:07.597
the stronger the marketing,
the weaker the sales.

00:10:07.597 --> 00:10:10.607
- Now, quickly define moats in terms of

00:10:10.607 --> 00:10:13.857
a business that keeps competition away.

00:10:15.177 --> 00:10:17.763
- Well, you know, if you talk to

00:10:17.763 --> 00:10:19.701
Michael Porter, he would give you

00:10:19.701 --> 00:10:22.951
five books on what is meant by strategy

00:10:24.825 --> 00:10:27.797
and competitor advantage
and durable competitor

00:10:27.797 --> 00:10:29.166
advantage, and if you talk to Warren

00:10:29.166 --> 00:10:30.733
and Charlie they would just say

00:10:30.733 --> 00:10:33.494
it is a moat, and they'd
break it down to one word,

00:10:33.494 --> 00:10:37.129
but basically it's the
ability of a business

00:10:37.129 --> 00:10:41.952
to have some type of an
enduring competitive advantage

00:10:41.952 --> 00:10:45.507
that allows it to earn
a better-than-average

00:10:45.507 --> 00:10:49.807
rate of return over an
extended period of time,

00:10:49.807 --> 00:10:52.434
and so some businesses have narrow moats,

00:10:52.434 --> 00:10:54.406
some have broad moats, some have moats

00:10:54.406 --> 00:10:56.560
that are deep but get filled up

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:58.627
pretty quickly, so what you want

00:10:58.627 --> 00:11:00.701
is a business that has a deep moat

00:11:00.701 --> 00:11:02.806
with lots of piranha in it

00:11:02.806 --> 00:11:05.367
and that's getting deeper by the day.

00:11:05.367 --> 00:11:07.367
That's a great business.

00:11:08.466 --> 00:11:10.306
I'm flexible, I think it's very

00:11:10.306 --> 00:11:13.159
important in investing
to be a learning machine,

00:11:13.159 --> 00:11:15.718
and to have flexibility and to be

00:11:15.718 --> 00:11:18.885
willing to look at the opportunity set

00:11:21.652 --> 00:11:24.214
and decide whether you
need to do anything at all

00:11:24.214 --> 00:11:26.098
or what is the best thing you can do

00:11:26.098 --> 00:11:28.291
based on the available opportunities.

00:11:28.291 --> 00:11:31.228
Let's say you have come up with a

00:11:31.228 --> 00:11:33.456
compelling value proposition.

00:11:33.456 --> 00:11:35.692
Let's say you're absolutely truthful,

00:11:35.692 --> 00:11:37.741
you truly understand it, and your pitch

00:11:37.741 --> 00:11:39.668
is truthful and all of that.

00:11:39.668 --> 00:11:42.585
What I have found is that basically

00:11:47.171 --> 00:11:50.006
whatever you come up with, and

00:11:50.006 --> 00:11:52.033
you've come up with it in your lab

00:11:52.033 --> 00:11:54.419
and thinking about it, talk with friends,

00:11:54.419 --> 00:11:58.586
looks great, the service,
the product looks great.

00:11:59.696 --> 00:12:02.200
The real litmus test of that is

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.271
when you put it in front of customers.

00:12:05.271 --> 00:12:08.071
So when you go out and make your pitch,

00:12:08.071 --> 00:12:10.083
your kit and ad and whatever else,

00:12:10.083 --> 00:12:12.004
and the very, very important thing for a

00:12:12.004 --> 00:12:16.598
entrepreneur to do is when
they put that out there

00:12:16.598 --> 00:12:20.394
be very, very, pay a
lot of close attention

00:12:20.394 --> 00:12:23.316
to the feedback you get from the people

00:12:23.316 --> 00:12:24.433
who are listening to the pitch,

00:12:24.433 --> 00:12:26.795
and this is very important
at the front end.

00:12:26.795 --> 00:12:29.500
Because what is probably the case

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:34.225
is that whatever you came
up with, is off base.

00:12:34.225 --> 00:12:38.128
It's not exactly what your market wants.

00:12:38.128 --> 00:12:40.545
And I'll give you an example.

00:12:41.504 --> 00:12:44.610
There's a friend of mine in Chicago

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.729
and he started this company
started InstallShield,

00:12:47.729 --> 00:12:49.891
and many of you might be familiar

00:12:49.891 --> 00:12:51.235
with the company and you're installing

00:12:51.235 --> 00:12:53.323
different windows products,
InstallShield comes up,

00:12:53.323 --> 00:12:55.823
and Viresh basically was like,

00:12:56.775 --> 00:12:58.931
I think 19 years old or something

00:12:58.931 --> 00:13:01.974
when he came up with InstallShield,

00:13:01.974 --> 00:13:04.187
and he said, he went to a trade show,

00:13:04.187 --> 00:13:05.864
he was going to go to a trade show,

00:13:05.864 --> 00:13:08.554
some software tools trade show,

00:13:08.554 --> 00:13:09.387
and he was trying to do

00:13:09.387 --> 00:13:10.736
a whole bunch of different things, he was

00:13:10.736 --> 00:13:13.289
trying to GoogleMaps 30 years ago,

00:13:13.289 --> 00:13:16.094
when there was no way that the

00:13:16.094 --> 00:13:18.395
computing could support
that sort of thing.

00:13:18.395 --> 00:13:20.296
But he went to this trade show

00:13:20.296 --> 00:13:22.081
and he had created this kind of

00:13:22.081 --> 00:13:25.767
trade show booth with
the different products

00:13:25.767 --> 00:13:28.727
and he had seven products, seven

00:13:28.727 --> 00:13:31.271
different tools that he was pitching,

00:13:31.271 --> 00:13:34.354
and it wasn't balancing on that booth

00:13:35.482 --> 00:13:37.493
'cause he wanted to put
four bullets on one side

00:13:37.493 --> 00:13:39.246
and four bullets on the other

00:13:39.246 --> 00:13:41.576
and he was at like four and three

00:13:41.576 --> 00:13:43.635
so it was really bothering him

00:13:43.635 --> 00:13:46.965
and so, as an afterthought,
this little tool he had

00:13:46.965 --> 00:13:48.883
was just to install software,

00:13:48.883 --> 00:13:51.433
for installing software
was his eighth bullet.

00:13:51.433 --> 00:13:54.086
He just put that at the end, the bottom,

00:13:54.086 --> 00:13:57.211
and it will install a
software installation tool.

00:13:57.211 --> 00:14:00.375
And through all of the whole week goes by,

00:14:00.375 --> 00:14:01.371
I think it was just a couple of days

00:14:01.371 --> 00:14:02.471
he was at this trade show booth

00:14:02.471 --> 00:14:03.561
and all of these people are coming by

00:14:03.561 --> 00:14:04.992
and all this stuff going on, and there

00:14:04.992 --> 00:14:08.687
was a guy across the aisle from him

00:14:08.687 --> 00:14:11.818
who spent two days looking
at those eight things

00:14:11.818 --> 00:14:15.369
because he was in the software business

00:14:15.369 --> 00:14:16.852
and after two days he came up to him

00:14:16.852 --> 00:14:18.292
and said you know, that product,

00:14:18.292 --> 00:14:19.721
I am interested in that product.

00:14:19.721 --> 00:14:20.785
And this product did not exist,

00:14:20.785 --> 00:14:23.085
it was in Viresh's mind, and so

00:14:23.085 --> 00:14:24.889
this was one of the only leads

00:14:24.889 --> 00:14:26.442
he'd picked up at the show,

00:14:26.442 --> 00:14:27.964
so he went back and created the product

00:14:27.964 --> 00:14:29.553
and gave it to the guy, and then

00:14:29.553 --> 00:14:31.183
he found that other people wanted

00:14:31.183 --> 00:14:32.552
it, and so on, and so forth.

00:14:32.552 --> 00:14:36.104
So bottom line is, if you study startups,

00:14:36.104 --> 00:14:38.771
you will find over and over that

00:14:39.818 --> 00:14:44.542
what actually works is not
what you actually came up with.

00:14:44.542 --> 00:14:45.880
What actually works is that, you know,

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:49.640
some tangent or some kind
of, you mentioned something

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:52.249
to a customer in passing
and then they grab

00:14:52.249 --> 00:14:53.993
onto that, say I want that, and you say

00:14:53.993 --> 00:14:56.177
oh, he's unusual, he wants unusual thing,

00:14:56.177 --> 00:14:58.195
I'll still keep my core pitch: no.

00:14:58.195 --> 00:14:59.455
What you really have to do is pay

00:14:59.455 --> 00:15:02.914
really close attention
to what is going on.

00:15:02.914 --> 00:15:04.131
- Thank you so much for watching.

